<div class="vs-hero">
  <h1>Plastic Surgery: China vs Korea (2026)</h1>
  <p>South Korea — Seoul's Gangnam district especially — is the world's plastic-surgery capital. China is the rising value alternative. Here's an honest, side-by-side comparison to help you decide.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>💉 Cost Comparison: Common Procedures</h2>
  <table class="vs-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Procedure</th><th>🇨🇳 China</th><th>🇰🇷 Korea</th><th>🇺🇸 USA</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Rhinoplasty</strong></td><td class="win">$3,000–$6,000</td><td>$3,000–$8,000</td><td>$8,000–$15,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Double Eyelid Surgery</strong></td><td>$1,500–$3,000</td><td>$1,000–$3,000</td><td>$4,000–$7,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Breast Augmentation</strong></td><td class="win">$4,000–$7,000</td><td>$6,000–$12,000</td><td>$10,000–$20,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Facelift</strong></td><td class="win">$4,000–$8,000</td><td>$8,000–$18,000</td><td>$12,000–$25,000</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Liposuction (per area)</strong></td><td class="win">$1,500–$3,000</td><td>$2,000–$5,000</td><td>$4,000–$8,000</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note">Prices are 2026 estimates for total procedure cost. Korea's premium Gangnam clinics can price well above these midpoints; China's Grade 3A hospitals sit at the lower end. Always confirm a per-case quote.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🏥 Quality, Volume &amp; Brand</h2>
  <p><strong>South Korea</strong> has the world's highest per-capita plastic-surgery rate and the strongest international brand, with Gangnam's clinic density unmatched anywhere. Korean surgeons are world leaders in <strong>Asian facial procedures</strong> — double eyelid surgery, facial contouring, and Asian rhinoplasty — and clinics are built around international patients with multilingual coordinators.</p>
  <p><strong>China</strong> is a strong value alternative. Board-certified surgeons at Grade 3A hospitals such as PUMCH and the Plastic Surgery Hospital of CAMS often hold international fellowships and perform high volumes. China's particular strengths are <strong>revision and reconstructive surgery</strong> and complex facial contouring, at lower prices. The trade-off is a younger international brand and fewer English-first dedicated clinics.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🎯 Which Should You Choose?</h2>
  <div class="verdict">
    <div class="box china">
      <h4>Choose China if…</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Price is the priority — 30–50% lower for major procedures</li>
        <li>You need revision or reconstructive surgery</li>
        <li>You want hospital-based oversight (JCI / Grade 3A)</li>
        <li>You're combining surgery with another treatment or check-up</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="box other">
      <h4>Choose Korea if…</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the world's strongest plastic-surgery brand and experience</li>
        <li>Asian facial refinement (eyes, nose, contouring) is the goal</li>
        <li>An English-first, tourism-oriented clinic matters</li>
        <li>Maximum clinic choice and patient reviews are priorities</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Korea leads on brand, volume, and refined Asian facial techniques; China leads on price and revision/reconstructive strength. The right choice depends on whether you optimize for the proven tourism experience or the lowest cost with accredited oversight.</p>
</div>

<div class="vs-card">
  <h2>🛂 Visa &amp; Logistics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>South Korea:</strong> K-ETA or visa depending on nationality; short flight from East Asia.</li>
    <li><strong>China:</strong> an L (tourist/medical) visa is standard, and some nationalities qualify for 240-hour transit visa-free. See the <a href="/visa-guide.html" style="color:#1e3c72;font-weight:600">visa guide</a> for every option.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

